# Normalize line endings. The generated canonical artifacts are byte-compared
# by --check-canonical, so they must check out identically on every platform.
# Windows' default autocrlf would hand them to the drift check with CRLF and
# fail every fresh clone.
* text=auto
docs/reference/canonical-replacements.json text eol=lf
docs/reference/canonical-replacements.md text eol=lf

# ############################################################################
# WHEN dist/ CONFLICTS DURING A MERGE OR REBASE: REBUILD IT.
# NEVER KEEP EITHER SIDE.
#
#     npm run build:action && git add dist/
#
# ############################################################################
#
# dist/ is GENERATED by `npm run build:action` (ncc). Neither side of a
# conflict here is authoritative, so "resolving" it by picking one is not a
# resolution at all: it looks clean, and quietly ships a stale bundle.
#
# This is proven, not theoretical. It happened on 3fe92ad, where a rebase
# conflict in dist/normwind.mjs was resolved in favour of the upstream copy.
# The commit went red on all seven CI legs. Note also that the bundle can
# change even when the source edit looks like it could not possibly affect it:
# that commit only removed an already-unused helper, but it was the second user
# of a name, and the minifier stopped emitting that name once one user was
# left. So never skip the rebuild on the grounds that the diff looks
# irrelevant.
#
# The prepush check "action: committed bundle is current" does catch a stale
# bundle, but only on the commit that carries it, which need not be the commit
# that looked conflicted. That is exactly how 3fe92ad shipped: the rebuild
# landed on a LATER commit, so the two were green together locally and the
# earlier one was red the moment it was pushed alone.
#
# `-merge` makes git refuse to invent a merge result here. A three-way textual
# merge of two single-line minified bundles is meaningless, and the failure
# mode is silent corruption. With `-merge`, git conflicts, leaves the current
# branch's copy in the worktree, and writes NO conflict markers, so there is no
# half-merged bundle that could be committed by accident. It only triggers when
# both sides changed dist/; when one side did, git still resolves normally.
dist/** text eol=lf -merge

# The regression suite (scripts/test-regression.mjs) does a plain string
# comparison of fixture input/output against the committed expected.* files.
# On machines with core.autocrlf=true, a fresh checkout would materialize
# input.* with CRLF while expected.fixed.*/expected.fixall.* stay LF (or vice
# versa), failing the byte comparison for reasons unrelated to the code under
# test. Pin every fixture file to LF regardless of local autocrlf settings.
test/fixtures/** text eol=lf
